    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom centred on the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, ruled by 43 kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a>, dated to 999, to the Fulani jihad.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> (1463–1499) built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a>, founded the Kurmi market and took advice on Islamic rule from al-Maghili.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">Indigo</a>-dyed cloth, leather, kola and the caravan trade made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> the leading market city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells, later the everyday small change of Hausaland, first reached the region in the reign of Muhammad Sharefa (1703–1731).</p></aside>
    </section>

