    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> grew up around Dala hill, a rocky outcrop on the savanna plain of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, where communities of farmers, hunters and ironworkers lived long before there was a kingdom. Almost everything known of its origins comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a>, an Arabic history of the kings of Kano written down in the late 1880s or early 1890s from older records and oral tradition, and translated into English by H. R. Palmer in 1908. The Chronicle tells of Barbushe, priest of a god called Tchunburburai who dwelt in a sacred tree, and of the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a>, grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> culture hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a>, who became the first king. Palmer dated Bagauda’s accession to 999 by counting the reigns back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> conquest, so the early dates are approximate at best, and the Chronicle itself records a dispute over who arrived first. By its account the third king, Gijimasu, or in the version it prefers his son Tsaraki, founded a walled town with eight gates at Gazarzawa below Dala, and Naguji, reckoned to have ruled from 1194 to 1247, was the first to collect a land tax of one-eighth of the crop. The newcomers’ rule was long resisted: the stronghold of Santolo held out until the fourteenth century, and the old cult survived longer still.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>’s wealth rested on the farmland of the surrounding plain and on crafts and commerce. Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, and the city walls enclosed fields as well as houses, so that Kano could feed itself under siege. Cotton cloth woven and dyed with indigo became its best-known product; in the traditional division of roles among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a>, Kano and Rano were the ‘kings of indigo’. Tanned goatskin was another export, and much of the ‘Morocco leather’ later prized by European bookbinders came from Hausa cities such as Kano. The Chronicle traces the growth of long-distance trade: under Abdullahi Burja (1438–1452) roads were opened from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> to Gonja, the source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> in what is now Ghana, and the king became the first in Hausaland to own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>; a prince from Bornu founded the market of Karabka; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> established the Kurmi market, which still trades today. Merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a> came to the city, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> settled there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached the court under Yaji (1349–1385), when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangara</a> Muslims from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> arrived and supplied an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and a judge, but it spread gradually, and many rural Hausa, the Maguzawa, kept the old religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was widespread: captives worked royal estates, and slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> served as soldiers and officials.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> fought with armoured cavalry and defended itself with walls. The Chronicle credits Kanajeji (1390–1410) with introducing quilted armour (<em>lifidi</em>), iron helmets and coats of mail after heavy losses in a war against the Umbatu, and records that he sent horses to the Kwararafa in exchange for slaves. Cavalry led Kano’s wars against its neighbours: Kanajeji defeated Zazzau and killed its king, and Rumfa’s war with Katsina lasted eleven years without a victor. Under Muhammad Kisoki (1509–1565) Kano attacked Birnin Nguru, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> army that came in reply could not take the city. The great earthen walls, begun by the Chronicle’s account in the late eleventh or twelfth century and extended by Rumfa and again around 1620 under Muhammad Nazaki, enclosed farmland and markets as well as houses and gave refuge in long sieges. They did not always hold. The Kwararafa, or Jukun, of the Benue valley ravaged the countryside in the reign of Muhammad Zaki (1582–1618), battered down a gate around 1653 and around 1671 broke into the city, killing many of its leading men while the king fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a>. Wars with Katsina, Zamfara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> filled much of the following century. The Chronicle records guns arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> under Kumbari (1731–1743), and Babba Zaki (1768–1776) was the first king with a guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa.jpg" alt="Gidan Rumfa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_to_the_Gidan_Rumfa_(2009)_in_Kano,_Nigeria..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonathan Riddell; Flickr jriddell; jriddell (Flickr), CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sarki ruled through a hierarchy of titled officials. Princely titles such as Dan Buram and Dan Isa preserved the names of men whom the Chronicle lists among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a>’s companions, while great offices such as Galadima and Madawaki carried command of the capital, the army and the provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> (1463–1499), whom the Chronicle calls the most powerful of all its kings, reshaped this system. It credits him with twelve innovations, among them a new palace, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a>, still the emir’s residence; the Kurmi market; an extension of the walls; the council of nine senior office-holders, the Tara ta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>; the seclusion of royal women (<em>kulle</em>); the public celebration of the Id festival; and the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to offices of state, including the treasury. Rumfa also sought guidance on Islamic government. The North African scholar Muhammad al-Maghili, almost certainly the Chronicle’s Abdu Rahaman, came to Kano in the 1490s, urged him to build a Friday mosque and cut down a sacred tree, and wrote for him a treatise on the duties of kings, <em>Taj al-din fi ma yajib ‘ala al-muluk</em>. Royal women could wield great power: Rumfa’s widow Madaki Auwa helped her son Abdullahi and her grandson Kisoki to rule. Later kings multiplied levies, and the Chronicle condemns Muhammad Sharefa (1703–1731) for seven new exactions ‘all of which were robbery’.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-amina"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-amina.jpg" alt="Queen Amina" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/queen-amina">Queen Amina</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Amina_Statue_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sidhant Bendre, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> became the leading commercial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, linking the kola country of Gonja and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> valley with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and the trans-Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. Its dyed cloth was worn far beyond Hausaland, and its markets drew merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, Bornu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a>. The kingdom was also a centre of Islamic learning: scholars came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, Bornu and North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and al-Maghili’s advice to Rumfa became a reference point for Islamic government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, quoted three centuries later in a work attributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>. Kano’s institutions outlived its Hausa dynasty. The Fulani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who took power in the early nineteenth century kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a>, much of the old title system and the walled city, and Kano remained the chief market of the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a>, which names forty-three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii">Muhammad Alwali</a>, is one of the richest sources for precolonial West Africa; among much else it places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-amina">Queen Amina</a> of Zazzau, who in its account conquered as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> and the Kwararafa, in the 1420s and 1430s, although other traditions and historians date her to the sixteenth century. The city walls are on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>’s tentative list for World Heritage status, and Kano is one of the largest cities of West Africa.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaria.jpg" alt="Zaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zazzau_Emirs_Palace_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aminucrus, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>’s fortunes swung through the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Defeats by Katsina, Jukun invasions and famine weakened it, and in the eighteenth century Katsina’s markets at times overshadowed its own. The Chronicle records that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> army camped outside the city in Kumbari’s reign, and that Kumbari’s heavy levies on the Kurmi market and on the townspeople nearly ruined the market and drove Arab merchants to Katsina. Pastoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> clans, taxed through the <em>jangali</em> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> levy since Kutumbi’s reign (1623–1648), had become an important part of the population, and some of their leaders sat on the council of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii">Muhammad Alwali II</a>, king from 1781, faced famine and quarrelled with his own officials. After war broke out in 1804 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> and the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, Fulani leaders in Kano, among them Muhammadu Bakatsine of the Jobawa, rose against Alwali in the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. Many histories date the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom to 1805. The Chronicle says that Alwali fought the Fulani for three years before he was driven from the city, an event Palmer dated to 1807, and that he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, was turned away and was killed at Burum-Burum. A young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, Sulaimanu, became the first Fulani emir, and Kano became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> until the British took the city in February 1903.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kings from around 1000 until the Fulani jihad of the early nineteenth century, built a walled city whose cloth, leather and markets made it the commercial heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> is one of the richest sources for precolonial West African history.</em></p>

